# Engineering Rules

## Tech Stack
- Vue 3 + Vite + TypeScript for frontend.
- Node.js + Express + TypeScript for backend.
- MySQL 8 + Prisma for database.
- Tailwind CSS for styling.
- Redis + BullMQ for background jobs.

## Architecture
- Use a monorepo with apps/web and apps/api.
- Separate controllers, services, repositories, and integrations.
- Keep business logic out of UI components.
- Validate all API inputs using Zod.
- Use environment variables for secrets.
- Never hardcode credentials.

## Business Rules
- One order can contain multiple shoes.
- Each shoe has independent inspection, price, photos, and status.
- Shoes requiring risk approval cannot enter washing before consent.
- Completed inspections establish final price automatically; no mandatory separate price agreement. Record staff verification, never fabricate customer approval. Preserve historical agreements/payments; validate old FINAL eligibility without backfill. Risk consent remains explicit.
- Rejected shoes have zero cleaning fees.
- Order status must be derived from its items when applicable.
- Every status transition must be validated and logged.
- Payment status is separate from laundry status.
- Customer tracking must use an unpredictable access token.

## WhatsApp
- Never send WhatsApp messages directly from controllers.
- Use a notification queue and provider adapter.
- Prevent duplicate notifications.
- Record delivery attempts and failures.
- Initial mock development fulfilled. Operational WhatsApp uses only whatsapp-web.js (owner-authorized). Fakes belong only in tests; preserve historical mock records without replay.

## Quality
- Enable TypeScript strict mode.
- Add tests for critical business workflows.
- Never silently swallow errors.
- Do not remove existing features without approval.
- Do not modify database schema without a migration.
- Do not claim a feature works without validating it.

## Development Workflow
1. Read .roo/docs/PRD.md before implementation.
2. Plan changes and identify affected modules.
3. Implement one feature at a time.
4. Run typecheck, lint, and relevant tests.
5. Summarize changes, tests, and known limitations.
